## 5.4 Factory-level access policies

Example policies:

sql

```sql

-- FACTORY ACCESS
create policy "Users view assigned factories"
on public.factories
for select
to authenticated
using (
  public.is_super_admin()
  or public.has_factory_access(id)
);

-- TRANSACTION ACCESS
create policy "View assigned factory transactions"
on public.stock_transactions
for select
to authenticated
using (
  public.is_super_admin()
  or public.has_factory_access(factory_id)
);

-- PRODUCTION ACCESS
create policy "View factory production"
on public.production_orders
for select
to authenticated
using (
  exists (
    select 1
    from public.stock_transactions t
    where t.id = production_orders.transaction_id
      and (
        public.is_super_admin()
        or public.has_factory_access(t.factory_id)
      )
  )
);

-- STOCK BALANCE ACCESS
create policy "View assigned factory stock"
on public.stock_balances
for select
to authenticated
using (
  public.is_super_admin()
  or public.has_factory_access(factory_id)
);

-- STOCK MOVEMENT ACCESS
create policy "View assigned factory movements"
on public.stock_movements
for select
to authenticated
using (
  public.is_super_admin()
  or public.has_factory_access(factory_id)
);
```

For the remaining tables, create equivalent factory-scoped policies. Documents must be accessible only when the linked transaction belongs to an authorized factory.

Security rules:

- Users must not be able to assign themselves an administrator role.
- Users must not approve their own stock transactions.
- Users must not change approved transaction lines.
- Users must not directly insert stock movements.
- Users must not directly modify stock balances.
- Only approved database functions may post stock.
- Audit logs must not be editable by ordinary users.
- Never put the Supabase service-role key in Flutter.

These policies are a security foundation, not the complete production policy set. Production deployment requires testing every table, operation and role, including cross-factory access.

# 6\. Bill and document management

Your system needs to store two types of bills:

1. Customer-provided bills.
2. Factory-generated or supplier-provided bills.

Use a private Supabase Storage bucket named:

`wayamba-documents`

Recommended file path:

text

```text
factory-id/
    transaction-id/
        customer-bill.pdf
        delivery-note.pdf
        supplier-invoice.jpg
        weighbridge-ticket.jpg
```

Document fields:

| Field | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| Document ID | Unique identifier |
| Transaction ID | Linked stock transaction |
| File name | Original file name |
| File path | Storage location |
| Document type | Customer bill / supplier bill |
| Uploaded by | Employee |
| Uploaded at | Upload timestamp |

Use short-lived signed URLs for authorized viewing and downloads. Apply Storage policies to both upload and read operations, checking the factory membership through the transaction ID. Restrict file size, MIME types and permitted upload paths.

The original document should be preserved even if a transaction is later cancelled.

