## Workflow C – Inter-factory transfers

This needs special attention.

mermaid

```mermaid

flowchart TD
 A[Factory A Creates Transfer] --> B[Enter Materials and Quantities]
 B --> C[Attach Transfer Bill]
 C --> D[Manager Approval]
 D --> E[Dispatch Material]
 E --> F[Move Quantity to In Transit]
 F --> G[Factory B Confirms Receipt]
 G --> H[Manager Reviews Receipt]
 H --> I{Approved?}
 I -->|Yes| J[Add Actual Quantity to Factory B]
 I -->|No| K[Investigate Difference]
```

A material transfer is not a customer sale.

For example:

- Factory A sends 3,000 kg.
- Factory B receives 2,950 kg.
- Difference: 50 kg.

The system must record the 50 kg discrepancy for investigation. It must not silently add 3,000 kg to Factory B.

# 9\. Financial and valuation rules

I recommend keeping three separate valuation concepts.

| Valuation | Formula | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Production value | Quantity × selling price | Production performance |
| Inventory value | Quantity × weighted average cost | Stock valuation |
| Dispatch value | Quantity × dispatch price | Sales/dispatch reporting |

Example:

| Description | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Production quantity | 1,500 kg |
| Selling price | Rs. 310 |
| Production value | Rs. 465,000 |
| Manufacturing cost | Rs. 270/kg |
| Production cost | Rs. 405,000 |
| Gross potential margin | Rs. 60,000 |

The Rs. 60,000 is a potential margin estimate, not actual net profit. It excludes other relevant expenses.

This separation will make your monthly management reports much more useful.

